        "text": "Training deep neural networks (NN) is generally a highly non-convex problem. The loss landscape of a NN is generally very rugged, with the number of local minima growing rapidly with model size (Bray & Dean, 2007; S\u0327ims\u0327ek et al., 2021). Yet, recent work has found many interesting structures in the loss landscape (Kunin et al., 2021a; Garipov et al., 2018). It has been observed that many NN loss landscapes contain approximately flat directions along which the loss does not change significantly (Freeman & Bruna, 2017; Garipov et al., 2018). The loss landscape is shaped by the model architecture and the dataset. One source of flat directions in the loss landscape is parameter transformations that keep the loss invariant (i.e. symmetries). Therefore, moving from a local minimum in the direction of a symmetry takes us to another minimum.\nWe show that continuous symmetries of the parameter space introduce many flat directions in the loss landscape, resulting in extended local minima. It has been hypothesized that gradient descent (GD) prefers flat local minima (He et al., 2019) and that wider local minima may generalize better (Hochreiter & Schmidhuber, 1997; Keskar\net al., 2017). Flat minima have been used to make ensemble or mixture models by sampling different parameter configurations that yield similar loss values (Garipov et al., 2018; Benton et al., 2021) which improves model performance. However, finding such flat directions is mostly done empirically (Freeman & Bruna, 2017; Garipov et al., 2018; Draxler et al., 2018; Benton et al., 2021; Izmailov et al., 2018), with few theoretical results. One result by Kunin et al. (2021a) relates some flat directions to scaling and translational symmetries in the loss function.\nIn principle, continuous symmetries of the loss result in flat directions in local minima. Here we derive a general class of continuous symmetries in NN. Our key insight is to focus on equivariances of\nthe nonlinear activation functions. We show that most known continuous symmetries of NN can be derived using this framework. However, models related by such symmetries are exactly equivalent, meaning they cannot behave differently on different inputs. Hence, we argue that for ensembling or robustness task, we need to find data-dependent symmetries. Indeed, aside from the familiar \u201clinear symmetries\u201d of NN, the framework of equivariance allows us to introduce a novel class of symmetries which act nonlinearly on the parameters and are data-dependent. These nonlinear symmetries cover a much larger class of continuous symmetries than the linear symmetries. They define continuous symmetries for almost any activation function and involve much larger continuous groups than most known linear symmetries. We show theoretically that such nonlinear symmetries may improve model performance on out of distribution (OOD) samples. We also provide preliminary experimental evidence that ensembles using these nonlinear symmetries are more robust to attacks.\nExtended flat minima arise quite frequently in the loss of NNs. We show that symmetry-induced flat minima can be parametrized using conserved quantities. We discuss a way to derive such conserved quantities and show how it is related to the work on Noether\u2019s theorem (Kunin et al., 2021a; Tanaka & Kunin, 2021). We also derive the explicit form of conserved quantities for different continuous symmetries of NN. Our contributions can be summarized as follows:\n1. Using equivariance, we present a general framework for finding symmetries in NN. 2. We introduce a new class of nonlinear, data-dependent symmetries in nonlinear NN. 3. We show that continuous symmetries in NN can lead to flat minima in the loss landscape,\nwhich can be traversed by applying symmetries. 4. We devise a procedure for deriving conserved quantities (CQ) associated with symmetries. 5. We show that symmetry-induced flat minima can be parametrized using the associated CQ.\nThis paper is organized as follows. First, we review existing work on flat minima, continuous symmetries of parameter space, and conserved quantities. In sec. 1 we begin by defining continuous symmetries. Next, we define flat minima and show how linear symmetries lead to extended flat minima. Then we work out a few examples of linear symmetries in NN. After that, we introduce nonlinear, data-dependent symmetries. In sec. 2 we introduce a formalism for deriving conserved quantities for parameter space symmetries and show its relation with Noether\u2019s theorem used by others. Then, we show how CQ can be used to define coordinates along flat minima. We close with some preliminary experiments involving nonlinear symmetries, conserved quantities and a discussion of potential use cases."
