ar transformed weights on a different batch. Consider F (x) = U\u03c3(V x). We ask whether the X-dependent transformed U, V in g \u00b7 (U, V,X) = (U\u0303(g, V X), gV,X) can replace the original U, V on data other than X . Specifically, if we use (U \u2032, V \u2032) = (Uc(g\u22121, gV X), gV ) to define F \u2032(x\u2032) = U \u2032\u03c3(V \u2032x\u2032), when is F \u2032(x\u2032) \u2248 F (x\u2032)? Using (13), we are asking when is c(g, Z)\u03c3(Z \u2032) \u2248 \u03c3(gZ \u2032) with Z = V X and Z \u2032 = V X \u2032. First, note that for any permutation X \u2032 = XP , (12) and (13) yield an exact symmetry because \u03c3(Z \u2032) = \u03c3(Z)P and c(g, Z)\u03c3(Z)P = \u03c3(gZ)P . Thus, on permutations of X even if the batch size k\u2032 of XP is larger than h, the nonlinear action is an exact symmetry. Next, assume X \u2032 is close to X with X \u2032 = XP + \u03bbH for \u03bb \u226a 1 and normalized H \u2208 Rn\u00d7k\u2032 . When \u03c3 is pointwise and Lipschitz continuous, meaning |\u03c3(a) = \u03c3(b)| \u2264 \u03b7|a\u2212 b| with constant \u03b7 \u2208 R>0, we can find bound on \u2225F \u2032(x\u2032)\u2212 F (x\u2032)\u2225 \u223c O(\u03b7\u03bb) (Appendix E).\nAdapting models to OOD using nonlinear symmetries. Consider the same F (x) = U\u03c3(V x) model. (assume \u03c3(Xi) = \u03c3(X)i acts on each column Xi). We want to find the parameters \u03b8\u2032 such that for a new data point X \u2032 \u2208 Rn\u00d7k\u2032 , the loss meaniL(\u03b8\u2032, X \u2032i) \u2248 meaniL(\u03b8, Xi). We now show this can be done if X \u2032 is related to X and \u03c3 satisfies the equivariance (13). Denote Z = V X and Z \u2032 = V X \u2032. Assume X \u2032 is such that Z \u2032 = gZP , where P \u2208 {0, 1}k\u00d7k\u2032 is a permutation acting on samples in X (i.e. \u2211k j=1 P j i = 1). Note that because P is a permutation and \u03c3 acts column-wise, \u03c3(XP ) = \u03c3(X)P . We want to find U \u2032 such that U \u2032\u03c3(V X \u2032) = U\u03c3(V X)P . If \u