        "heading": "3 DISCUSSION OF APPLICATIONS",
        "text": "We ran a limited set of experiments aimed at assessing the utility of the nonlinear group action and conserved quantities. Some of the results are shown in Fig. 3. We find that Q can impact convergence rate and generalizability. We also find the nonlinear action to be viable for ensemble building to improve robustness under some adversarial attacks.\nConvergence rate and generalizability. Conserved Q are fixed at initialization. We find that values of Q can influence the convergence rate or the sharpness of the trained model. In Appendix M, we derive the relation between Q and convergence rate for two additional example optimization problems. We show that initializing parameters at certain Q values accelerates convergence. In Appendix N, we derive the relation between conserved quantities and sharpness of minima in a simple twolayer network, and show empirically that Q values affect sharpness (and possibly generalizability) in larger networks. While Q is often unbounded, common initialization methods such as (Glorot & Bengio, 2010) limit the values of Q to a small range (Appendix L). As a result, only a small part of the minimum is reachable by the models. Symmetries allow us to explore portions of flat minima which gradient descent rarely reaches.\nEnsemble models. Applying the nonlinear group action allows us to obtain an ensemble without any retraining or searching. We show that even with stochasticity in the data, the loss is approximately unchanged under the group action. The ensemble has the potential to improve robustness under adversarial attacks (Appendix O).\n7The polar coordinate are found by setting Usl = rU,s cos\u03d5U,s and Usk = rU,s sin\u03d5U,s (same for V T )."
